date-released: "2026-10-03"
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.23071166
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.23071166
    description: "Concept DOI (all versions)"
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.23128059
    description: "Version DOI for v0.5.0"
license: AGPL-3.0-only
repository-code: "https://github.com/sparkainlp-x/oes-resilience"
url: "https://sparkainlpx.xyz"
abstract: >-
  Open, reproducible benchmark for multichannel telemetry anomaly detection.
  Synthetic 512-channel signals are split into sixteen 32-channel blocks and
  scored by block-level detectors behind a plugin API: the transparent OES32
  reference detector (0.45 max|x| + 0.35 RMS + 0.20 mean|x|), a robust
  z-score baseline, EWMA and CUSUM temporal detectors, an oes32+ewma hybrid
  and an optional Isolation Forest, compared in a calibrated scorecard.
  Version 0.5.0 adds a NASA SMAP/MSL adapter (public data downloaded with
  per-file SHA-256 verification, never bundled) and a blind evaluation on
  that real telemetry under a protocol locked before any test-split scoring.
  OES32 did not meet its pre-stated success criterion: it beat only EWMA on
  SMAP, and the simpler max|x| and CUSUM baselines reached a higher pooled
  event F1 (not significantly).
  Version 0.4.0 added replay evaluation: a JSONL telemetry replay is scored
  against a hashed preregistration, with every detector's threshold calibrated
  by default to the same ~1% false-positive budget on a hash-locked,
  event-free calibration replay (fixed thresholds carry a fairness note).
  Version 0.3.0 added a seeded stress suite (heavy-tailed noise, missing
  channels, saturation and clipping, narrow and cross-block events, global
  shift, correlated blocks, drift and baseline steps) with robustness metrics
  (FP inflation, recall retention, Wilson intervals), explicit mask-aware
  handling of missing data, and honest negative results. The benchmark is
  synthetic; the only real data is the SMAP/MSL evaluation, which describes
  that dataset and protocol only. Not quantum, not GPS, not a physical sensor,
  and no performance claims about operational systems.
  Unreleased (concept): qbench, a classical, simulator-only evidence layer
  for small quantum-circuit benchmarks (preregistered plan, paired
  block-bootstrap comparison with metric and block sensitivity, evidence
  passport, optional SSH signatures) run on local Qiskit Aer simulators with
  a synthetic noise model. It has no hardware results and makes no quantum
  error correction, threshold or advantage claim.
